\cf2 \
I believe authenticity is a crucial word in all forms of work. While academic discourses, pedantic themes, or fine art related to contemporary politics always provide points to ponder regardless of the medium, my practice primarily revolves around translating the emotions I feel into images.
\f3\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs30 \cf2 Emotions are formless. Yet, in the process of translating them into images, one must confront these multidimensional emotions head-on before simply unraveling them visually. While visuals and aesthetics are excellent sensory tools that draw attention, I believe images produced solely for that purpose are dead. For an image to truly live, a narrative encompassing emotion, story, and context must operate prior to visual completion.
\f3\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs30 \cf2 The greater the intersection between the narrative running through the work and the audience viewing or consuming it, the more strongly the audience's heart is moved, leading to deep emotional involvement. I view this state in connection with what is called a 'cinematic experience' in film and moving images. Unlike the experience of simply following the flow of time in a movie, this refers to a state of emotional immersion generated between the image and the viewer.
\f3\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs30 \cf2 Although film and photography both possess a crucial temporality as mediums, their natures are vastly different. In film, temporality involves the actual progression of events and time through continuous images; the viewer has no choice but to follow the flow of time created by the director. Even if a film proceeds in a non-linear structure, time is merely rearranged, and cinematic temporality still exists in a different operational way.
\f3\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs30 \cf2 The temporality of photography, however, finely fragments and preserves time. When viewing a single image (frame) resulting from this process, temporality is generated subjectively by the viewer. To summarize: the time of film/moving images flows, while the time of photography exists in a compressed and suspended state\'97yet this time is reconstructed within the viewer's experience.
\f3\fs24 \cf0 \

\f2\fs30 \cf2 This is why I chose the medium of photography. Photography halts a single moment, but the emotions and narratives contained within are not fixed. Rather, within that suspended image, the viewer's own time and experience intervene, and the meaning of the image is constantly regenerated. I believe that through this point\'97where a still image begins to move again within the viewer\'97I can most densely address multidimensional emotions.}